---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const groups = [
  {
    id: 'user',
    title: 'User & problem',
    items: [
      'Who exactly is the user of this piece of UI, and what job are they trying to get done?',
      'What evidence do we have (research, support tickets, analytics) that this is a real problem?',
      'What does success look like, and how will we measure it after shipping?',
      'What happens if we build nothing? Is there an existing path users already take?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'domain',
    title: 'Domain & data',
    items: [
      'What are the entities and their states? Have we listed the edge cases (zero, one, many, too many)?',
      'What are the empty, loading, partial, error, and permission-denied states?',
      'What data volumes are realistic (10 items or 10,000)? Does the design survive both?',
      'Who is allowed to see or do this? How do we render for users without permission?',
      'Are there compliance, privacy, or audit requirements touching this data?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'surface',
    title: 'Surface & platform',
    items: [
      'Which surface(s) does this ship on (web app, marketing site, mobile web, native, embedded)?',
      'What input modes must work: touch, keyboard, pointer, screen reader, voice?',
      'What browsers/devices are in support, and does anything here need a fallback?',
      'Does this surface have its own theme, density, or constraints the system must respect?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'responsive',
    title: 'Responsive & layout',
    items: [
      'What does this look like at 320px wide and at 400% zoom, with no horizontal scrolling?',
      'Which breakpoints/container sizes change the layout, and are they system tokens?',
      'Should this respond to its container (container queries) rather than the viewport?',
      'How does it behave with long content (translation expansion, long names, wrapped text)?',
      'Are touch targets at least 24×24 CSS px (larger for primary actions)?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'reuse',
    title: 'Reuse & one-offs',
    items: [
      'Do we already have a component or pattern for this? Have we actually searched the docs and Figma library?',
      'If it almost fits: is the gap a variant, a composition, or genuinely new semantics?',
      'Is this a one-off? If so, it lives in the product. What would promote it to the system later (a second consumer)?',
      'If we build it locally, who is accountable for it, and have we recorded the decision?',
      'Are we about to fork/detach a system component? What misfit is driving that, and has the system team heard it?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'api',
    title: 'Component API & architecture',
    items: [
      'What is the prop contract (variants, sizes, states)? Are names consistent with the rest of the library?',
      'Controlled, uncontrolled, or both? Who owns the state?',
      'Composition or configuration, and does the choice match how consumers will actually use it?',
      'Does it extend native element attributes and forward refs?',
      'Which tokens does it consume? Any hard-coded value a theme should own?',
      'What is explicitly out of scope for v1, and is that written down?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'a11y',
    title: 'Accessibility',
    items: [
      'Which ARIA APG pattern (if any) applies, and are we following its keyboard spec?',
      'Can the entire task be completed with keyboard alone? Where does focus go on open, close, and error?',
      'Does every element have an accessible name, role, and state? Is color never the only signal?',
      'Do text and UI contrast ratios pass AA in every theme?',
      'How are async updates announced (live regions)? Is motion gated on prefers-reduced-motion?',
      'Who runs the screen reader pass, and with which pairing (NVDA/Chrome, VoiceOver/Safari)?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'content',
    title: 'Content & internationalization',
    items: [
      'Do we have real copy (labels, errors, empty states), or are we designing around lorem ipsum?',
      'Do button labels say what they do, and does the vocabulary match the rest of the product?',
      'Will this be translated? Does the layout survive 2× text length and RTL?',
      'Are dates, numbers, and plurals formatted via Intl, not string concatenation?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'states',
    title: 'States & interaction detail',
    items: [
      'Is every state designed (hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading, error, empty, overflow)?',
      'When does validation fire, and where do errors appear and get announced?',
      'Optimistic or pessimistic updates? What does the user see during latency, and on failure?',
      'Is the action reversible? Undo, confirmation, or neither, and why?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'apis',
    title: 'APIs & integration',
    items: [
      'Is the data contract agreed with the backend (shapes, nullability, error codes)?',
      'How do loading, partial, and failure responses map to UI states?',
      'Pagination, caching, and refresh: who owns them, and what does stale data look like?',
      'Is this behind a feature flag? What is the rollout and rollback plan?',
      'Any rate limits, timeouts, or offline behavior the UI must expose honestly?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'ai',
    title: 'AI interfaces (when relevant)',
    items: [
      'The output is non-deterministic. How do we set expectations, and what does a wrong answer cost the user?',
      'What are the loading/streaming states? Can users read, stop, or interrupt generation in progress?',
      'How do we show provenance (sources, citations, confidence) so users can verify rather than trust?',
      'What are the refusal, empty, and failure states, and do they explain what to try instead?',
      'Can users correct, edit, regenerate, or undo AI output? Is a human always in control of consequential actions?',
      'How is feedback captured (thumbs, reports), and where does it go?',
      'What data leaves the user\'s context to power this, and is that disclosed?',
      'Does latency match the interaction (streaming for long generations, honest progress otherwise)?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'quality',
    title: 'Performance & quality',
    items: [
      'What is the bundle/asset cost, and is it acceptable for this surface?',
      'Any layout shift, image sizing, or font loading issues (Core Web Vitals)?',
      'What analytics events prove the success metric from question one?',
      'What is the test plan (unit, visual regression, automated a11y, manual passes)?',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'tradeoffs',
    title: 'Trade-offs & alignment',
    items: [
      'What are we trading (speed vs completeness, flexibility vs consistency), and is the choice explicit?',
      'What is the deadline forcing us to cut, and is that debt recorded with an owner?',
      'Who signs off (design, a11y, engineering), and have they seen this list?',
      'Where is the decision recorded so the next person doesn\'t re-litigate it?',
    ],
  },
];

const total = groups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.items.length, 0);
---

<Base title="Pre-code checklist">
  <h1>Pre-code checklist</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The questions to answer before anyone writes code. Use it live in a conversation with a PM,
    designer, or engineer. Skip groups that don't apply, but skip them on purpose. Progress is
    saved in this browser.
  </p>

  <div class="progress" role="group" aria-label="Checklist progress">
    <div class="progress-row">
      <span class="progress-count" id="progress-count" aria-live="polite">0 / {total}</span>
      <div class="progress-bar" aria-hidden="true"><div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div></div>
      <button class="reset-btn" type="button" id="reset-checklist">Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    groups.map((group) => (
      <details class="check-group" open>
        <summary>{group.title}</summary>
        <ul>
          {group.items.map((item, i) => (
            <li>
              <label>
                <input type="checkbox" data-check-id={`${group.id}-${i}`} />
                <span>{item}</span>
              </label>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Not every question needs a long answer. "Not applicable, because…" is a valid answer. The
      failure mode this list prevents is the question nobody asked.
    </p>
  </div>

  <script>
    const KEY = 'ds-checklist-v1';
    const boxes = Array.from(
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>('input[data-check-id]')
    );
    const countEl = document.getElementById('progress-count');
    const fillEl = document.getElementById('progress-fill');
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('reset-checklist');

    function load(): Record<string, boolean> {
      try {
        return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) ?? '{}');
      } catch {
        return {};
      }
    }

    function save(state: Record<string, boolean>) {
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(state));
      } catch {
        /* storage unavailable; checklist still works for the session */
      }
    }

    function render() {
      const done = boxes.filter((b) => b.checked).length;
      if (countEl) countEl.textContent = `${done} / ${boxes.length}`;
      if (fillEl) fillEl.style.width = `${(done / boxes.length) * 100}%`;
    }

    const state = load();
    for (const box of boxes) {
      box.checked = Boolean(state[box.dataset.checkId ?? '']);
      box.addEventListener('change', () => {
        state[box.dataset.checkId ?? ''] = box.checked;
        save(state);
        render();
      });
    }

    resetBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      for (const box of boxes) box.checked = false;
      save({});
      render();
    });

    render();
  </script>
</Base>
